# Why `include` Does Not Work Inside an Echo Statement in PHP

You cannot echo the output of a file inclusion by wrapping the `include` statement or the file path inside `echo`. The code snippets you provided will simply print the literal text `<?php include ('http://www.example.com/script.php'); ?>` to your browser, rather than executing the remote script.

This behavior stems from how PHP parses source code versus how it handles output generation. When you use `echo`, you are instructing the interpreter to treat the string inside the quotes as static data. PHP does not evaluate PHP tags that appear within double-quoted strings or single-quoted strings unless those tags are escaped or handled by specific functions like `eval()`.

Attempting to bypass this with raw string concatenation, such as:
```php
echo "include (\"http://www.example.com/script.php\");";
```
results in the same outcome. The interpreter sees the quotes immediately following `echo`, recognizes them as a string delimiter, and outputs exactly what is inside those delimiters without executing any embedded logic.

## The Security Implications of Remote Inclusion

The fact that this approach fails is not merely a limitation of the language; it is a critical security feature. Including remote files directly from your application code via `include` or `require` is notoriously dangerous and often violates strict Content Security Policies (CSP) enforced by modern browsers and server configurations.

When you attempt to `include` a URL like `http://www.example.com/script.php`, PHP treats it as a remote resource fetch. However, most servers block such requests at the network level or prevent them via configuration directives like `allow_url_include`. Furthermore, including external scripts creates an immediate supply chain risk. If that remote server is compromised or serves malicious payloads, your application executes them with your server's privileges.

Modern development practices generally discourage fetching PHP code from the internet to render it. Instead, resources are served as static assets (HTML, CSS, JavaScript) or consumed via APIs and rendered in a controlled manner within your own templates.

## The Correct Way to Output Remote Content

If your goal is to display the contents of a remote file in your response, you must fetch the raw bytes first and then output them explicitly. This approach separates the retrieval logic from the execution logic, keeping your application secure and predictable.

Using cURL or the HTTP client functions is the standard method for this task:

```php
$url = 'http://www.example.com/script.php';

// Initialize the session
$ch = curl_init();
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_URL, $url);
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, true); // Fetch instead of outputting immediately
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION, true); // Follow redirects if necessary

$response = curl_exec($ch);
$httpCode = curl_getinfo($ch, CURLINFO_HTTP_CODE);
curl_close($ch);

// Check for success before echoing
if ($response !== false && $httpCode === 200) {
    echo $response;
} else {
    // Handle errors appropriately (e.g., HTTP 403, network timeout)
    echo 'Failed to retrieve content.';
}
```

This method ensures that the remote file is treated as a string variable (`$response`) rather than code to be interpreted by your server. You are essentially acting as a proxy or aggregator, displaying content fetched from another source without executing its internal logic on your own stack.

## Using eval() and Its Risks

The only function capable of interpreting a string containing PHP code is `eval()`. However, using `eval()` with remote input is widely considered an anti-pattern in secure software development.

```php
// DO NOT DO THIS
$content = file_get_contents('http://www.example.com/script.php');
eval($content); 
```

Even if you could technically make the previous code work by fetching and evaluating, doing so opens your application to arbitrary remote code execution (RCE). An attacker controlling the remote server could inject malicious code into `$content`, which would then execute directly on your server. This bypasses all standard input validation and sanitization layers because the payload runs as part of your own script's execution context.

## LaravelContext

If you are building a dynamic application where rendering external content is necessary, frameworks like [LaravelCompany](https://laravelcompany.com) provide robust utilities for handling data fetching and view rendering that encourage safer patterns over raw `eval` usage. They often offer Blade components or API integration helpers that abstract the complexity of data retrieval while maintaining strict boundaries around what gets rendered to the user.

In summary, PHP does not support echoing included files because it distinguishes between data strings and executable code. To display remote content safely, fetch it as a string using cURL or `file_get_contents()` and echo that variable. Never attempt to execute remote scripts directly within your application logic.
